WHY THIS LESSON IS IMPORTANT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Controlled elements are useful because:

• React State Controls Form Values
  - Form values can be read directly from component state

• Predictable Data Flow
  - State determines what the form displays

• Validation
  - Values can be checked as the user interacts

• Dynamic Forms
  - Fields can depend on other fields

• User Feedback
  - Character counts and validation messages can update immediately

• Easy Reset/Pre-fill
  - State can initialize or clear the form

• Submission
  - Current values are readily available to submit handlers

• Testing
  - User interactions can be tested through predictable state-driven UI


================================================================================
PART 2: UNDERSTANDING CONTROLLED ELEMENTS
================================================================================

WHAT IS A CONTROLLED ELEMENT?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A controlled form element is a form control whose important value is driven
by React state.

For text-based controls:

State
  ↓
value Prop
  ↓
Input


For checkboxes:

State
  ↓
checked Prop
  ↓
Checkbox


The user's interaction then updates that state through an event handler.


================================================================================
CONTROLLED INPUT FLOW
================================================================================

Initial State
     ↓
Component Renders
     ↓
Input Gets value={state}
     ↓
User Types
     ↓
onChange Fires
     ↓
Handler Reads New Value
     ↓
setState(newValue)
     ↓
React Processes Update
     ↓
Component Renders
     ↓
Input Receives Current State
     ↓
Cycle Repeats


================================================================================
BASIC CONTROLLED INPUT FORMULA
================================================================================

const [name, setName] =
  useState('')


<input
  value={name}
  onChange={(event) =>
    setName(event.target.value)
  }
/>


MEMORY:

STATE
  ↓
value
  ↓
INPUT
  ↓
onChange
  ↓
setState
  ↓
STATE


================================================================================
CONTROLLED VS UNCONTROLLED
================================================================================

+----------------------+---------------------------+----------------------------+
| ASPECT               | CONTROLLED                | UNCONTROLLED               |
+----------------------+---------------------------+----------------------------+
| Current value        | React state               | DOM element                |
| Reading value        | State                     | Usually ref/FormData       |
| Updating value       | State setter              | DOM handles editing        |
| value/checked        | Supplied by React         | Usually defaultValue/      |
|                      |                           | defaultChecked             |
| Real-time logic      | Straightforward           | Different approach         |
| Reset/pre-fill       | Update state              | DOM/ref/native mechanisms  |
| Typical use          | State-driven forms        | DOM-driven form scenarios  |
+----------------------+---------------------------+----------------------------+


IMPORTANT CORRECTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Controlled inputs are NOT universally "better" than uncontrolled inputs.

Both are valid patterns.

Controlled inputs are particularly useful when the application needs the
current value during rendering or needs state-driven behavior such as:

• Live validation

• Conditional fields

• Character counts

• Dynamic enabling/disabling

• Derived UI

• Programmatic value changes


Uncontrolled inputs can be appropriate when:

• The DOM can own the value

• Values are mainly needed at submit time

• Native form APIs/FormData fit the requirement

• Working with file inputs


