IMPORTANT PERFORMANCE NOTE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Controlled elements are not automatically faster than uncontrolled elements.

A controlled input generally causes state processing/rendering as its value
changes.

For ordinary forms this is usually perfectly reasonable.

For very large or performance-sensitive forms, architecture and form handling
strategy may matter.

Choose controlled inputs because they fit the application's data flow,
not because they are automatically a performance optimization.


================================================================================
IMPORTANT ACCESSIBILITY NOTE
================================================================================

Controlled state alone does NOT make a form accessible.

Accessibility also depends on:

• Correct labels

• Appropriate HTML elements

• Keyboard behavior

• Error associations

• Focus management

• Instructions

• Meaningful grouping

Examples:

<label htmlFor="email">
  Email
</label>

<input
  id="email"
/>


Radio group:

<fieldset>
  <legend>
    Choose an option
  </legend>

  ...
</fieldset>


